To solve the inequality $\frac{2}{3} < \frac{x}{5} < \frac{6}{7}$, we begin by multiplying all parts of the inequality by 5 to eliminate the denominator on the left side:

$$
\frac{2}{3} \cdot 5 < x < \frac{6}{7} \cdot 5
$$

Compute the values:

$$
\frac{10}{3} < x < \frac{30}{7}
$$

Now, we express these fractions as decimals to find the range of integer values $x$ can take.

$$
\frac{10}{3} \approx 3.333\ldots
$$
$$
\frac{30}{7} \approx 4.2857\ldots
$$

So, we have:

$$
3.333\ldots < x < 4.2857\ldots
$$

The only integer $x$ in this interval is 4.

