To find the matrix that represents a projection onto the y-axis, let's analyze the projection of a point $ (x, y) $ onto the y-axis. When projecting a point onto the y-axis, the x-coordinate becomes 0, and the y-coordinate remains the same. This transformation can be represented by the matrix:

$$ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} $$

To verify this, let's consider the effect of this matrix on a vector. If we have a vector $ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} $, multiplying by the matrix:

$$ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \cdot x + 0 \cdot y \\ 0 \cdot x + 1 \cdot y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{pmatrix} $$

This is indeed the x-coordinate being set to 0 and the y-coordinate remaining the same, which is the definition of projecting a point onto the y-axis.

